## Context

The user runs agent sessions in several projects at once, mostly inside subagents the terminal barely shows. They need one trustworthy place that says, per project, which session is working, in which pipeline and phase, and whether it waits on them, and that replays what past sessions did. Two agent sessions in one project overwrite each other's specs, plans and commits, so the same place must keep them from running together.

Four facts shape the design. The pipeline's current state already lives in the project's files, which record their own status and progress; anything derived elsewhere can only disagree. What the files lack (who ran a phase, when, from which prompt, in what order, for which pipeline) exists only as the agent reports it, and reports are best effort. An agent cannot report its own crash or closed terminal, but Claude Code runs hooks at every session start, prompt, tool call, stop and session end; other hosts have none. And the project's repo must stay clean of anything the observer keeps.

A session waiting an hour on the user still holds the project; one that exited does not, yet its end hook can be killed before it writes, as archiving a desktop session showed. The host does close a finished conversation's transcript with a record of its own. And an ended conversation can be resumed, but only the project's latest session knows its current state; an older one coming back after a newer session worked carries a stale picture.

## Strategy

**The observer is reached at session start and one restart only.** The agent first reports the session start, whose reply names its observer session, then calls start, which reuses the machine's one observer or launches it on the user's port, then a short fixed list in order, recording the port taken. Every later report lands first in its session's log outside the project, then is posted. A missed post never blocks but prompts one restart; no port, at start or restart, blocks, since an unseen session breaks the single-source promise.

**One live session per project, decided at start from disk.** Start judges the project's other sessions from presence, ended marks and transcripts, never from an observer that may have replayed nothing, counting one that never reported its start. If one is open, start halts naming it, its state, last activity, pipeline and phase, until the user approves ending it, never the caller's own, or closes this session. One it finds closed by its transcript it marks ended, so a later resume meets the reopen rule rather than slipping back in.

**Presence comes from the host, not the agent.** Claude Code's hooks mark a session working on start, prompt and tool call, waiting when the agent stops, and ended when it ends. Each call writes presence first, joined to the observer session by the start report's reply and keeping the transcript's location, then the ended mark, then pings a running observer, never failing the agent. The end hook may still be killed unwritten; hosts without hooks fall back to reported events.

**Liveness is judged read-only, in a fixed order.** An ended mark, then an end hook, means ended. Failing both, a transcript whose last record is the host's closing record means closed without reporting its end, but only when that record names the presence's own conversation; a resume writes past it and reads live again. Otherwise the last hook state holds, quiet and maybe stuck past 15 minutes, never ended. A missing or unrecognised transcript changes nothing; a crash leaves no record, so only the user's approval releases it.

**Ends are reversible, for the most recent session only.** A session is the user's conversation, recognised again when resumed in a fresh process. A report from an ended one is recorded and told it was previously ended and to run the start check; a report alone reopens nothing. The check clears the mark only when no session of the project started later and none is open. A newer session, or an end by another's approval, is a hard stop: that conversation's picture is stale, so the user continues in a new one.

**Pipelines are reported explicitly; rails resume from kept logs.** A pipeline is one feature flow from its starting stage to completion or abandonment, named by its board task when it has one. The agent reports its start, finish and every skipped phase with the reason, and every phase, gate, step and QA report names it, since a rail cannot tell a skip from a missed report. The live session shows one rail for its current pipeline; resuming it marks done what earlier sessions' kept logs finished.

**Files decide state; the parts meet only in the observer.** Status is rebuilt on every event and page load from the project's files, session logs, each session's liveness and the board, all handed to it: files decide current state, events add who, when, history and stages, and liveness is never re-judged. The board is the kanban folder the project's brain config declares, read through the one brain-config reader, under the kanban tag from the newest session-start report. Records, board and status never depend on each other, so a failure empties only its own part.

## Good examples

**Archived, then resumed too late.** The user archives a session waiting on them; its end hook is cut off, but its transcript ends in the closing record for that conversation. The next session's start judges it closed, marks it ended and proceeds with no halt. Days later the user resumes the archived conversation: its report is recorded and flagged, its start check finds the newer session and hard-stops, and the user carries on in a new session.

**Reopened as the most recent.** A session reaches the plan gate and the user exits; the end hook marks it ended. Next morning they resume it, no other session having started since. Its first report is recorded with "previously ended"; the start check finds none newer and none open, clears the mark, and the session shows working, its history running across the end in order.

**A crashed session found by the next one.** A build session's terminal crashes mid-step: no end hook fires and no closing record is written, so forty minutes later it reads quiet and maybe stuck. A new session's start halts naming it, its pipeline and the Build phase. The user approves ending it; it is marked ended with its log kept, and the new session resumes the pipeline with Spec through Plan already done on its rail.

## Bad examples

**Liveness from the wrong signal.** A session that exited counts as live because its last report is recent; one waiting on the user an hour is judged ended because its transcript has gone quiet; or a just-restarted observer that has replayed nothing lets a second session in while presence on disk shows the first working.

**A stale conversation slips back in.** A session judged closed from its transcript is left unmarked while a newer one builds and ends. The old conversation is resumed in a fresh process, reports as if it were new, finds nothing open and plans from a picture the newer session's commits have changed; or its reports are refused and it works on unobserved.

**Running unobserved.** The observer dies and the restart finds every port busy; the agent shrugs because reports are best effort and builds on, so the user's one source of truth shows a stale session while work lands in the project.

